# React Web Client Rules

## State & Effects
- Avoid `useEffect`. Only use it as a last resort for genuinely stepping outside React's core paradigm — i.e. synchronizing with external systems (DOM APIs, subscriptions, browser events, third-party non-React libs).
- Prefer derived/reactive state, `useMemo`, `useRef`, and event handlers over `useEffect` for anything that can be expressed as a reaction to render or user interaction.
- Never use `useEffect` to sync state that can be computed directly during render.

## Data Fetching
- Never use raw `react-query` (`useQuery`/`useMutation`) directly, except for calling `invalidateQueries`/`queryClient` methods.
- Always use the auto-generated Orval hooks/clients under `clients/` for data fetching and mutations — do not hand-write query functions or duplicate typings.
- If a needed endpoint isn't generated yet, regenerate the client rather than bypassing it.

## Styling & UI
- Design mobile-first: base styles target small viewports, use Tailwind responsive prefixes (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`) to scale up.
- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes as the default styling approach; avoid custom CSS unless Tailwind can't express it.
- Use shadcn/ui components as the base building blocks; extend/compose them rather than rebuilding equivalents from scratch.
- Add tasteful microinteractions (hover/focus/active transitions, loading/skeleton states, subtle animations) for interactive elements, keeping them performant and accessible.

## Error Handling
- Handle errors explicitly at the boundary where they occur (query/mutation error states, form validation, API failures) — never swallow errors silently.
- Use error boundaries for render-time failures and toast/inline messaging for async/user-action failures.
- Always provide a fallback UI state (empty, loading, error) for any data-dependent component.

## General
- Prefer composition and small, single-responsibility components over large monolithic ones.
- Keep components typed strictly — no `any`; leverage types generated by Orval where applicable.
- Co-locate component-specific logic/hooks/styles rather than scattering related code across the tree.